@@ -6,10 +6,11 @@
 
 ## Abstract
 
-Object hallucination scores such as CHAIR and object F1 are reference-based quality measures whose reference, a gold object set, is usually incomplete. This registered audit tests whether their verbosity slope (F1 on log object count) is ordered from one reference to all references to verified labels, and whether references omit objects randomly. On 600 Crossmodal-3600 images with Vietnamese references (six systems, one backbone), the registered ordering held: −8.99 [−10.89, −6.99] with one reference, −2.43 with all 2–3 references and +19.32 [+17.43, +21.13] under human-verified Open Images labels (95% image-bootstrap intervals, conditional on the systems; in effect a short-versus-detailed contrast). This label gold is smaller than all references, with another precision rule. A second backbone (Qwen3-VL-8B) reproduced it on 537 of them (clean subset). On PASCAL-50S the registered replication failed at its label-gold step (−2.29 [−5.15, +0.61]). On 3,226 reserved COCO images with four LVLMs, one registered look found one-reference gold below a size-matched random-deletion null, as on PASCAL-50S (here from the label gold: −26.79; null 95% range [−8.76, −6.69]), consistent with selective omission; the label-gold slope exceeded the one-reference slope by 35.46 points, partly expected from its 2.149-fold size. Two post hoc checks bound it: the positive label-gold sign depends on excluding unverifiable mentions, and the reference-gold signs rest on one verbose system. Nine registered predictions failed (two of five on Crossmodal-3600). In public human judgments relevance falls with object count, but count-matched precision tracks it worse than raw precision.<!-- rev4 (A_SPIC_2026-11-03 META_REVIEW item 3b, 4a-c, 7a, 9f, 13; 2026-11-04 writer, proposal for the PI rewrite): claim restated as one reference -> all references -> verified labels; X5 leads the COCO sentence; AD-1' written as a slope difference, partly size-expected (2.149 = tier3.json analysis.premise.L_over_R1