diff --git a/paper/cover_letter_A.md b/paper/cover_letter_A.md
index 78e13a7..6eb1d0d 100644
--- a/paper/cover_letter_A.md
+++ b/paper/cover_letter_A.md
@@ -10,7 +10,7 @@ Dear Editor,
 We submit the manuscript "Gold Completeness and the Verbosity Slope of Object Hallucination Metrics: A Registered Audit" for consideration as an original research article in *Signal Processing: Image Communication*.
 
 Object-hallucination metrics such as CHAIR and object F1 are widely used to rank captioning and vision–language systems. They are reference-based quality measures of image descriptions, and their reference is the gold object set, which is usually incomplete. The manuscript asks when these quality measures measure what they claim, and tests this with pre-registered analyses.
-- **Main result.** In registered tests on Vietnamese Crossmodal-3600 images (intervals conditional on the systems scored), and with a second Qwen backbone on 537 of the same 600 images, the slope of object F1 on log object count is ordered from one reference to all references to verified labels (six systems of one backbone); there the label gold is smaller than all references and uses another precision rule. Its positive sign under human-verified labels depends on excluding mentions the labels cannot verify, and the reference-gold signs rest on one verbose system (both post hoc). Two of five registered Crossmodal-3600 predictions failed. The registered primary replication on PASCAL-50S (50 references per image, seven public LVLMs) failed at its label-gold step (50 references against VOC labels: −2.29 [−5.15, +0.61]). A post hoc count (no verdict) found fewer VOC-label than reference-named class instances (1,787 vs 2,162), consistent with that failure; the slope still rose at every registered reference level (secondary), and a registered companion test placed reference gold below a size-matched random-deletion null. On 3,226 reserved COCO val2017 images with four LVLMs not used in the Crossmodal-3600 label-gold tests, a registered one-look test passed: one-reference gold fell below a size-matched random-deletion null from the label gold, and the label-gold slope exceeded the one-reference slope by +35.46 [+34.19, +36.56] points, a step partly expected from the label gold's measured size; against five references the label gold did not meet its registered size premise, so that comparison is not claimed. The full ordering claim is therefore scoped to Crossmodal-3600 and its second backbone (Section 6.4, Table 1). All registered verdicts, failures included, are listed in one table.
+- **Main result.** In registered tests on Vietnamese Crossmodal-3600 images (intervals conditional on the systems scored), and with a second Qwen backbone on 537 of the same 600 images, the slope of object F1 on log object count is ordered from one reference to all references to verified labels (six systems of one backbone); there the label gold is smaller than all references and uses another precision rule. Its positive sign under human-verified labels depends on excluding mentions the labels cannot verify; with those mentions settled by a human annotator the slope stays positive on both backbones (H1a PASS; single annotator; Section 6.7.2), and the reference-gold signs rest on one verbose system (both post hoc). Two of five registered Crossmodal-3600 predictions failed. The registered primary replication on PASCAL-50S (50 references per image, seven public LVLMs) failed at its label-gold step (50 references against VOC labels: −2.29 [−5.15, +0.61]). A post hoc count (no verdict) found fewer VOC-label than reference-named class instances (1,787 vs 2,162), consistent with that failure; the slope still rose at every registered reference level (secondary), and a registered companion test placed reference gold below a size-matched random-deletion null. On 3,226 reserved COCO val2017 images with four LVLMs not used in the Crossmodal-3600 label-gold tests, a registered one-look test passed: one-reference gold fell below a size-matched random-deletion null from the label gold, and the label-gold slope exceeded the one-reference slope by +35.46 [+34.19, +36.56] points, a step partly expected from the label gold's measured size; against five references the label gold did not meet its registered size premise, so that comparison is not claimed. The full ordering claim is therefore scoped to Crossmodal-3600 and its second backbone (Section 6.4, Table 1). All registered verdicts, failures included, are listed in one table.
 - **Other findings.** In public human judgments, relevance falls with object count, but count-matched precision tracks it worse than raw precision. A registered count-matched leaderboard failed its fidelity check and was not scored, so no count-control result is confirmatory; the re-scoring of 15 published comparisons is post hoc.
 
 The paper fits the journal's scope on multimedia quality assessment and measures: it treats an object-level caption score as a reference-based quality measure of a visual description, shows that its validity depends on its reference set, the gold, and continues the journal's discussion of whether image-captioning metrics measure what they should.
