 ## 8.1 What the results say together
 
-**Gold ladder (confirmatory).** Object F1's verbosity slope is ordered from one reference to all references to verified labels on XM3600 (six systems of one backbone) and, with a second backbone, on 537 of the same 600 images (Sections 6.2–6.3). The reference steps are low-risk because the golds are nested (Section 4.2); the label step, a change of completeness plus precision rule (Table 6.2), is the informative one, and its tally is mixed: PASS on XM3600 under verified precision, PASS on the second backbone under the registered precision rule (under the error-counting rule its interval includes 0: +1.97 [−0.44, +4.01]), and FAIL on PASCAL-50S (Section 6.4). There the registration assumed that the VOC labels are more complete than 50 references; a post hoc count without model outputs found 1,787 VOC label instances against 2,162 classes named in the 50 references (ratio 0.83; no verdict), consistent with a label level that was not more complete, and the registered FAIL stands as registered. The ordering claim therefore rests on XM3600 and the second backbone. **Which objects references name (registered).** A registered companion test found the reference-gold slope below a size-matched random-deletion null on PASCAL-50S (X4) and on COCO val2017 (X4, X5; Section 6.6), consistent with reference gold penalising verbose captions through which objects people name, not only how many; it is not proof of cause. **Count:** no confirmatory claim (Section 3.3).<!-- A-T3 tier 3 (2026-11-01): COCO val2017 X4/X5 PASS = data/results/a_t3/tier3.json (5735bd20f26b) :: analysis.{X4,X5}.verdict; the COCO sentence of this paragraph was cut for the A-T3 400-word cap (repeats §6.4) --> **Human judgments and preference data:** humans penalize object count on long captions, count-matched precision tracks them worse than raw precision, and public preference data carry a count channel (Section 7).<!-- rev2 (CC-181 W6, CC-182): G0 = data/results/a_g0/g0.json (sha256 23e12cbd179e) :: S_voc_voc