+Object-level hallucination scores respond to the gold and to the number of objects a caption names, not only to object choice. In a registered out-of-sample test on XM3600, and with a second Qwen backbone on 537 of the same 600 images, F1's verbosity slope is ordered from one reference to all references to verified labels (six systems of one backbone, in effect a short-versus-detailed contrast); its positive sign under human-verified labels holds only when unverifiable mentions are excluded; with those mentions settled by a human annotator the slope stays positive on both backbones (H1a PASS; single annotator; Section 6.7.2), and on XM3600 the reference-gold signs rest on one verbose system (both post hoc). Nine registered predictions failed (Table 1), among them the label-gold part of the PASCAL-50S replication, the all-references sign on XM3600 and both count-matched human-agreement predictions; one gold-size value missed its interval, four secondary predictions were inconclusive and one PASCAL-50S companion prediction was not evaluable. Seven predictions were registered on the labels (single annotator): five passed (H1a, H1b, H1c, H2b, H3), one failed (H2d), none was inconclusive and one was not evaluable (H2a) (Table 1). For H2b (single annotator), the budget was smaller than the number of unknown mentions for only one of the twelve systems; for the other eleven, coverage holds by construction (measured effective floor 0.24 to 0.42). No count-control claim is confirmatory. These results concern these Qwen-VL systems, two families of English LVLM outputs and, on PASCAL-50S, seven public LVLMs. On COCO val2017, with four LVLMs not used in the XM3600 label-gold tests, one-reference gold fell below a size-matched random-deletion null, and of the ordering only the label-over-one-reference step is supported (label gold against five references: premise not met).<!-- src: data/results/n_f1/bracket_result.json (sha256 4796ed12) :: outcome_paper = ii; sets.xm600.slopes.l