 **Length and count as confounds.** LeHaCE (Wei et al., 2024), the closest prior work in method, fits a model-specific curve of hallucination against description length and evaluates every model at a uniform length. It also reports the slope of that curve as a model-level metric, computed under one gold per data set; Section 6 reports that the sign of a related slope, that of object F1 on the number of mentioned objects, can change with the gold. Hallucinated objects concentrate late in a caption (LURE; Zhou et al., 2024a), and image reliance fades during generation (M3ID; Favero et al., 2024); the matched prefix of Section 3.3 relies on this ordering. Closest in aim, Fazli et al. (2026) re-evaluate six inference-time mitigation methods and conclude that "current evaluation protocols may overestimate progress by rewarding conservative generation", and Sandhu et al. (2026) find that a targeted CHAIR reduction leaves captions shorter and more conservative and lowers object recall. Training-side work is summarised in Supplementary S9.<!-- cite: zhou2024analyzing (LURE); zhou2024aligning (POVID) is Zhou et al., 2024b; citation audit manuscript/review/citation_audit/intext_trace.tsv --><!-- A5.1: REVIEW_A A:51, A:52, A:58 SUPPORTED in manuscript/research_r15/VERIFY/verify.json; OPERA checked in its PDF (CHAIR with max new tokens 512 and 64). REVIEW_A:57 (VCD) is not used: arXiv_2311.16922.pdf contains no CHAIR evaluation --><!-- cite (moved from the text): LeHaCE = Wei, Wang, Zhang, Fan and Zhao, NeurIPS 2024, DOI 10.52202/079017-3538; SumGD = Min et al., Findings of NAACL 2025, DOI 10.18653/v1/2025.findings-naacl.235; Less is More = Yue et al., arXiv 2402.14545; OPERA = Huang et al., CVPR 2024, arXiv 2311.17911; LURE = Zhou et al., ICLR 2024, arXiv 2310.00754; M3ID = Favero et al., CVPR 2024, arXiv 2403.14003; Fazli et al. arXiv 2609.01888 (quote: abstract); Long et al. arXiv 2609.35294 (DOPA gold); Nguyen et al. arXiv 2610.14976 (IEEE Access per arXiv comment) --><!-- 