-**F-measures, thresholds and incomplete gold.** Lipton et al. (2014) prove that the F1-optimal decision rule adds an item when its probability of being correct exceeds half the attainable F1; Section 4's sign condition is their result. Saunders and Freitas (2022) note that on genuine positive–unlabeled data the F-measure cannot be calculated exactly and can serve only as an estimate that rests on the selected-completely-at-random (SCAR) assumption; tests of that assumption exist (Molotkov and Artomov, 2023; Teisseyre et al., 2024; S. Liu et al., 2025). Species-accumulation curves (Colwell et al., 2012; Chao et al., 2014) supply the expected gold size. Treating repeated samples as sampling occasions, species-richness estimators bound from below the number of classes that no sample has recorded (Chao, 1987; Chao and Colwell, 2017), and estimators of this kind have been applied to human-produced lists, to estimate the size of a set enumerated by crowd workers (Trushkowsky et al., 2013) and class cardinality in a knowledge graph (Luggen et al., 2019); Section 5.4 applies the incidence form pooled over (image, class) pairs, with an image's references as its sampling occasions. In information retrieval, incomplete relevance judgments bias evaluation measures (Buckley and Voorhees, 2004), and condensed lists without unjudged documents are more robust (Sakai, 2007; Sakai and Kando, 2008). With errors introduced into relevance judgments through a model of judge error, system orderings that statistical tests report as significant can be incorrect (Rashidi et al., 2024). In captioning, completeness acts through verbosity and can set the sign of F1's verbosity slope, not only the size of a bias.<!-- pass 3: Chen et al. (2015, §4.2) sentence removed here for length; the same contrast is stated in Section 4.1, where chen2015microsoft stays cited --><!-- cite: Colwell, Chao, Gotelli, Lin, Mao, Chazdon and Longino, J. Plant Ecology 5(1):3-21, DOI 10.1093/jpe/rtr044, verified 2026-